        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Boston</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1630 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founded</span><span class="info-stat-val">Founded in September 1630 by English Puritan settlers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a> and Isaac Johnson, who crossed to the peninsula on the invitation of William Blaxton, its first European resident; the Massachusett people had lived and fished in the region long before.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the Shawmut Peninsula and the shores of Boston Harbor, an arm of Massachusetts Bay, at the mouth of the Charles River in eastern Massachusetts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Puritan settlement of 1630; Boston <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> School and Boston Common; the Boston Massacre of 1770 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> of 1773; the siege of 1775 and 1776; the abolitionist movement; the Boston <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and the city’s literary life; the first subway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>; land made from tidal flats</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Boston is the capital and most populous city of the state of Massachusetts.</span></div>
