    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Puritan settlers took up Blaxton’s invitation and settled the Shawmut Peninsula in September 1630, and Boston became the largest town in the Thirteen Colonies until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> outgrew it in the mid-18th century.</li><li>Resistance to British taxes and troops produced the Boston Massacre of 1770 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a> Party of 1773, and the siege that followed ended with the British evacuation on 17 March 1776.</li><li>In the 19th century the city became a manufacturing and financial centre, took in Irish and other immigrants, led the abolitionist movement and tripled its area with landfill between 1631 and 1890.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>More than two-thirds of inner Boston’s modern land area did not exist when the city was founded: it was made by filling tidal areas, using earth from the levelled hills and gravel brought by train from Needham to fill the Back Bay.</p></aside>
    </section>

